Consult the SCORE User's Guide for the settings for SCREEN, MIDI, and IRQ. 1/8

LIBRary (\LIB\) and HELP (\HLP\) directories are usually found on disk C:.     
   However, it may be useful to have special libraries for certain projects
   or there may be some reasons to use different names for these directories.
   Example: To change drive name only to D, type: LIBR D:\SCOR4\LIB\
   To change directory name to LIB2 on drive E, type: LIBR E:\LIB2\   If you
   are using only drive C, the drive letter is not needed.  LIBR \PETERSLIB\
   will cause libraries to be read from the \PETERSLIB\ directory on the
   current logged drive. NO MORE THAN 13 CHARACTERS can be used for the
   complete directory path. The same procedures are used to change the HELP
   directory location. If a custom directory is created make sure that ALL
   original \LIB\ or \HLP\ files are first copied to the new directory BEFORE
   any altered or new files are added.

AFTC, AFTB, AFTK, and AFTM are used to alter the minimum space set by the
   the Justify command after a clef (AFTC), a bar line (AFTB), a meter number 
   (AFTM), or a key signature (AFTK) to the next note or rest. The minimum
   space created by these commands will be an absolute value, regardless of 
   the staff size. (The default minimum spaces vary with staff size. At size
   1, the space after a clef = 1.8, after a bar = 2.5, after a key sig. = 2.8,
   after a meter = c.3.7.) (The spacing of the LIneup command is independent
   of these commands.)

*
                                                                           2/8

GUITar is used to set the file name and path for the guitar chord grid data
   file. The default for this is X:\LIB\GUITAR.DAT, where "X" is the drive
   letter for the \LIB directory. However any name and path may be used for
   a file containing this data. Thus you may have specially prepared chord
   data for each project. See the Upgrade information for details regarding
   this data file.  NO MORE THAN 24 CHARACTERS MAY BE USED.

CODE9 is used to set the file name and path for the user-created Code 9 
   terms file. The default for this is X:\LIB\CODE9.DAT, where "X" is the 
   drive letter for the \LIB directory. However any name and path may be used 
   used for a file containing this data. Thus you may have specially prepared 
   Code 9 terms files for each project. See the Upgrade information for 
   details regarding this data file. NO MORE THAN 24 CHARACTERS MAY BE USED.


REST sets the type font for numbered whole rests. The default value is 6 
   (for the Music Number font, as used with meter numbers). Any font number 
   that is valid for CODE 10 numbers may be used here. For example, to use
   Helvetica Bold type: REST 1005   See the CODE 10 help for details.
   (The Reference Manual has an error on page 118.  Bodoni Bold and Bodoni
   Bold Italic use the font numbers 7 and 8.)
 
*    
                                                                           3/8

The BEAM tilt factor sets limits on the amount a beam will tilt at the 
   input stage. The default factor is 10. This causes the difference in 
   the heights of the ends of the beam (P4, P5) to be about 2 scale steps
   when the end points are about 10 horizontal space units apart. For 
   steeper beams use a higher number (perhaps 12 or 14); for generally
   shallower beams use 7 or 8. (All the tilts may be adjusted in Edit Mode
   at any time after the initial input.)

BMTH controls the basic beam thickness with Laser printer output. The default
   here is -1. That is, all beams will have one pixel removed from their 
   normal width. This default is set with 300 dpi printers in mind. When
   printing on 600 dpi or 1200 dpi machines you may want to set BMTH to -2 
   or -3 to get somewhat lighter beams. Also it is sometimes desireable to
   decrease beam thickness when printing orchestral scores where the staff
   size must be rather small. This variable is independent of a beam's P17,
   which is a factor that applies only locally to a single beam. (Use this
   to adjust the thickness of grace note beams, etc.)





*
                                                                           4/8

COLOr w,x,y,z sets the screen colors on VGA color displays.
   W = foreground (graphics), X = background, Y = text, Z = text while in
   edit mode. The defaualt values are 0, 15, 4, 9 (black graphics lines,
   white background, red text lines, blue text in edit mode). For white 
   lines on black background with yellow edit text use 15, 0, 15, 14.
   For colors similar to those used by the DOS editor, use 14, 1, 14,
   15 (yellow lines on blue background, white edit text.)  For more
   information see Users Guide, page xxxii.

DASH sets the width of dashes that are used to separate syllables in lyrics 
   under notes. The default size is 1.29.  The dashes may be changed in Edit 
   Mode, but if the CD (center dash) is used it will cause the dashes to 
   revert to the size given in the preference DASH statement.

XDASh can limit the number of dashes that will appear between syllables. The
   default of zero puts no limit on the number. In this case the number of 
   dashes is determined by the distance between the syllables.  If XDASH 
   is set to 1, then only 1 dash will appear, no matter how far apart the 
   syllables may be.  Other numbers may be used, but in any case, very close 
   syllables will never have more than 1 dash. (The CD command will always
   follow the current restriction set by XDASh.)

*
                                                                           5/8

DYNAmics (p, pp, f, fff, etc.) may be automatically displaced to left of 
   notes at Input time by giving a value to this variable. The default is
   zero.  DYNA -1 will displace marks 1 step to the left of a designated
   note, etc. A value of -.5 is good for most cases. (This variable does 
   not affect the position of any marks put in with the Nx feature. At Input 
   time, Nx will put in Code 9 library item "x" under the designated note.)

INCHes/METRic:  When the word INCHes or METRic appears in the preference 
   file the mode of measurements is established. (The measurement system 
   may be changed at any time by typing INCHes or METRic time while in 
   SCORE Command Mode.) 

MIDK sets the position of the special keys used on the MIDI keyboard for 
   step-time note input.  The default for this is C2.  If you have an
   extended keyboard range you should normally set this to C1, thus using
   the lowest octave of the keyboard for the special keys. (See the Upgrade
   information for details on MIDI input.)

PROXimity sets note input proximity mode off (=0) or on (=1). When proximity
   mode is on, notes will always be in the closest octave range possible --
   unless an octave num. is given or 'U' or 'J' is typed. (Upgrade Man. 3.01)

*
                                                                           6/8

OUTPut sets the output line for printing on PostScript laser devices.  Use
   1 for LPT1 (parallel), 2 for LPT2, 3 for serial (COM 1).  If OUTPut is
   left at zero, you will be prompted for the output device at print time.


DESC: = 1 = <escape> key is disabled while printing.  
      = 0 = <escape> key is enabled, allowing pause and escape from
            printing process. (See PRINT help for more details.)



PROCedures sets the limit on the number of lines of code reserved for 
   creating PostScript procedures at print time.  The default value is
   2000.  If your laser printer has less the 2 megabytes of memory and 
   you have down-loaded some extra fonts, it may be necessary to cut down
   on this limit. On the other hand, if your printer has much more memory,
   it may be possible to increase this value to as much as 5000.  If this
   value is increased, the tendency is that very full pages, with many 
   different Code 9 symbols will print a little faster.



*
                                                                           7/8
READ allows you to read in any preference file that you may have created for
   some special purpose.  Any name may be used for such a file (see below).

SAVE allows you to save whatever changes you may have made to the preference
   list during the current work session.  You may use any name for this
   saved file, however it is recommended that you use the name PREF. followed
   by some unique, distinguishing 3-letter extension. In this way preference
   files may be easily located in disk directories.  It is best that the name 
   PREF.SCR be reserved for the preference file you will most often be using.

RESOlution is used to give SCORE the proper dpi (dots/per/inch) inform-   
   ation about the laser printing device being used. This value is 
   necessary for the calculations made regarding the number of pixels 
   used for basic line widths.  The default is 300.

SETStroke is used to invoke special PostScript code that adjusts line
   placement on PostScript Level 1 printers.  When printing on 300 dpi 
   Level 1 printers some lines may appear thicker than others unless 
   SETStroke is set to 1. When SETStroke is active, compensation is made 
   for internal round-off errors.  In many cases the setting of 1 will 
   produce noticeably better copy -- however, the print time may be 
   increased by as much as 40%. (According to current information, 
   Level 2 PostScript takes care of this problem automatically.)
*
                                                                           8/8

MOUSE = 0 = mouse works as described in Manual Additions booklet, page 4.01.
      = 1 =   "     "   "     "       " Users Guide, page 15.

SLUR is used to set the basic thickness of slurs.  The default value is 1.
   .8 will produce somewhat thinner slurs.  When in Edit Mode, P13 may be 
   used to adjust the thickness of individual slurs.

TIES can be used to set the position given ties at Input time. The default
   setting is zero, which means that a tie will appear between the specified
   notes unless they are very close together - in which case the tie will 
   appear above or below the notes.  
   
   If TIES is set to 1, then the tie will always go above or below the notes 
   involved.
   
   If TIES is set to -1, then the tie will always go between the notes 
   involved.




